WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.440
It was the last Friday of the school year

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.800
and I had been actually subbing&nbsp;
for a teacher for three days.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.960
It just seemed like a nice day.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:18.180
On May 18th, 2018, a gunman entered a high school
in Santa Fe, Texas and opened fire.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.560
I look down and I realize in&nbsp;
my pants I had bloody holes&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.335
in my pants and I realized I'm shot.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.131
The gunman killed ten people.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.360
Flo Rice was shot six times.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.267
And then finally, Scot managed&nbsp;
to... he managed to find me.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.040
For four years, Flo and Scot have&nbsp;
told their story over and over&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.240
to push for new laws that&nbsp;
could prevent mass shootings.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.727
It’s made them part of a recurring&nbsp;
conversation on guns in the US.

00:00:52.727 --> 00:00:55.760
And a cycle the country has&nbsp;
seemed stuck in for decades:

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.000
a mass shooting, a push for&nbsp;
reform, and then no action.

00:01:00.538 --> 00:01:02.501
"...the tragic school shooting&nbsp;
in Santa Fe, Texas..."

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:03.634
"...this time in El Paso..."

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:05.124
"...nine people killed in Dayton..."

00:01:05.124 --> 00:01:07.370
"...how many years do we have to go through this?"

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:08.496
"...Congress is paralyzed..."

00:01:08.496 --> 00:01:10.268
"...we collectively seem to ask the same question."

00:01:10.268 --> 00:01:13.160
"What does it take to pass some&nbsp;
gun reform in this country?"

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.736
But here’s the thing:

00:01:14.736 --> 00:01:19.281
this cycle of inaction on gun&nbsp;
laws isn’t exactly accurate.

00:01:19.281 --> 00:01:23.873
Over the past few decades, federal&nbsp;
legislation on guns has been rare.

00:01:23.873 --> 00:01:28.730
But in state legislatures, mass&nbsp;
shootings have led to new gun laws.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:29.610
Thousands of them.

00:01:30.576 --> 00:01:34.147
...and how those laws have&nbsp;
emerged can tell us a lot&nbsp;

00:01:34.147 --> 00:01:38.002
about the future of guns in the US.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:48.200
In 2020, a study tried to determine “the&nbsp;
impact of mass shootings on gun policy.”

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.424
They looked at 25 years of&nbsp;
high profile mass shootings.

00:01:51.424 --> 00:01:54.557
Then, they looked at gun&nbsp;
legislation passed during that time.

00:01:54.557 --> 00:01:57.751
Over 3,000 laws across all fifty states.

00:01:57.751 --> 00:02:01.221
When they took a closer look at&nbsp;
those laws, a pattern emerged&nbsp;

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:03.831
that at first seemed unsurprising.

00:02:03.831 --> 00:02:08.951
State legislatures controlled by Democrats&nbsp;
were more likely to pass tighter gun laws.

00:02:08.951 --> 00:02:12.624
Republican-controlled states&nbsp;
typically loosened gun laws.

00:02:12.624 --> 00:02:15.000
But they found a key difference.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.286
Mass shootings didn't have any&nbsp;
statistically significant effect&nbsp;

00:02:18.286 --> 00:02:21.154
on the number of laws passed by Democrats.

00:02:21.154 --> 00:02:23.394
While for Republican legislatures

00:02:23.394 --> 00:02:26.096
a mass shooting roughly doubles the number of laws&nbsp;

00:02:26.096 --> 00:02:30.000
enacted that loosen gun&nbsp;
restrictions in the next year.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.348
To arm more teachers, for&nbsp;
example, or arm more school staff.

00:02:34.348 --> 00:02:37.856
That's James Barragan, a politics&nbsp;
reporter at the Texas Tribune.

00:02:37.856 --> 00:02:40.743
There is more access to guns afterwards.

00:02:40.743 --> 00:02:45.523
A state like Texas would go&nbsp;
more towards pro-gun policies&nbsp;

00:02:45.523 --> 00:02:47.860
in the aftermath of a gun shooting.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.767
Texas has some of the loosest&nbsp;
gun laws in the nation.

00:02:50.767 --> 00:02:54.143
And that matters — for&nbsp;
people all over the country.

00:02:54.143 --> 00:02:59.610
People probably don't know about the importance of&nbsp;
state gun laws and really state laws in general.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:04.533
Our gun laws at the federal level have been&nbsp;
frozen in time since basically the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.533 --> 00:03:08.077
which allowed the states to have&nbsp;
a much bigger role and a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.077 --> 00:03:12.540
bigger influence in how gun culture&nbsp;
played out in their jurisdictions.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.897
Let’s look at Texas.

00:03:14.897 --> 00:03:21.443
In 1991, a gunman killed 23 people at&nbsp;
a Luby’s restaurant in Killeen, Texas.

00:03:21.443 --> 00:03:25.467
A woman there named Suzanna Hupp lost&nbsp;
both her parents in the shooting.

00:03:25.467 --> 00:03:28.447
She believed she could have stopped the massacre

00:03:28.447 --> 00:03:32.410
and turned her experience into a&nbsp;
crusade for loosening gun laws.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.918
"I'm mad at my legislators for&nbsp;
legislating me out of the right&nbsp;

00:03:35.918 --> 00:03:38.285
to protect myself and my family."

00:03:38.285 --> 00:03:39.394
It worked.

00:03:39.394 --> 00:03:44.004
In 1994, Texas elected a&nbsp;
new governor: George W Bush

00:03:44.004 --> 00:03:48.034
who made it legal to carry a&nbsp;
concealed gun his first year in office

00:03:48.034 --> 00:03:51.854
and set off a trend in the state&nbsp;
that's continued for decades.

00:03:51.854 --> 00:03:55.210
For example in 2012, after&nbsp;
the Sandy Hook elementary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:58.613
school shooting drew attention&nbsp;
to gun laws across the country

00:03:58.613 --> 00:04:02.200
Texas responded a few months&nbsp;
later by creating a program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.193
allowing some school employees&nbsp;
to carry guns in school.

00:04:06.193 --> 00:04:12.730
In 2017, a gunman killed 26 people at&nbsp;
First Baptist Church in Sutherland Springs.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:17.698
Within two years, Texas made it legal&nbsp;
to carry weapons in places of worship.

00:04:19.253 --> 00:04:25.000
But after the Santa Fe High School shooting,&nbsp;
governor Greg Abbott did something unusual.

00:04:25.967 --> 00:04:28.946
He asked lawmakers to consider a “red flag law”

00:04:28.946 --> 00:04:31.203
which would allow authorities&nbsp;
to take firearms away&nbsp;

00:04:31.203 --> 00:04:33.730
from a person courts deemed dangerous.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:38.010
That is not something that&nbsp;
Republicans in this state often do.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:42.425
Flo and Scot were also pushing for&nbsp;
legislation in response to Santa Fe

00:04:42.425 --> 00:04:44.374
like laws that would hold&nbsp;
parents accountable

00:04:44.374 --> 00:04:47.567
if their guns were used by their children 
to harm people.

00:04:47.567 --> 00:04:50.823
They also pushed to make it&nbsp;
harder to buy ammunition online.

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:54.879
Our shooter, he just checked&nbsp;
the box and said, yes, I'm 18.

00:04:54.879 --> 00:04:56.880
And they delivered it to his doorstep.

00:04:58.657 --> 00:05:03.520
You can't get alcohol delivered without&nbsp;
showing proof of I.D. or something.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:04.880
But he ordered ammunition.

00:05:05.519 --> 00:05:09.174
Their hope for stricter laws was&nbsp;
in line with Texas public opinion.

00:05:09.174 --> 00:05:13.174
Polling showed only a small minority&nbsp;
of Texans supported loosening gun laws

00:05:13.174 --> 00:05:16.036
and just over half supported tightening them.

00:05:16.036 --> 00:05:19.543
We thought it was common&nbsp;
sense that this would be done.

00:05:19.543 --> 00:05:23.279
They came to Flo's hospital&nbsp;
room the week of the shooting.

00:05:23.279 --> 00:05:24.916
And we had the governor, lieutenant governor

00:05:24.916 --> 00:05:29.377
we had congressmen, we had senators,
their wives, the chief of staff

00:05:29.377 --> 00:05:32.747
all in her room at one time, at least 20 people

00:05:32.747 --> 00:05:35.295
and said, we're going to take care of you.

00:05:35.295 --> 00:05:37.684
We promise we'll be there for you.

00:05:37.684 --> 00:05:38.800
We'll fix this.

00:05:39.217 --> 00:05:44.010
But in the end, these proposals, along with&nbsp;
Abbot’s openness to red flag laws

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.180
went nowhere. 

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.073
After gun rights supporters went after him.

00:05:48.073 --> 00:05:49.550
The gun culture is strong.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:53.710
But the gun lobby itself also exerts a&nbsp;
lot of pressure on Texas politicians.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:58.356
There were bills that were put out there,&nbsp;
but they never made it out of committee.

00:05:58.356 --> 00:06:04.160
Later in 2019 — two shootings in west Texas&nbsp;
just weeks apart prompted Lieutenant Governor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:09.680
Dan Patrick to suggest another tighter gun&nbsp;
policy — closing background check loopholes.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.920
That is a very strong comment&nbsp;
from a lieutenant governor who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.931
is very pro-gun and who is&nbsp;
very friendly with the NRA.

00:06:17.931 --> 00:06:21.187
But Republican leaders were&nbsp;
saying we may have problems here.

00:06:21.187 --> 00:06:27.451
Democrats are pushing to take over the&nbsp;
state house for the first time since 2003.

00:06:27.451 --> 00:06:30.910
After elections were over, with&nbsp;
Republicans still in control

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:36.799
in 2021 Texas passed “constitutional carry”:
there would no longer be a requirement

00:06:36.799 --> 00:06:38.560
for Texans to have a license&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.760
or receive any training
to openly carry handguns.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:46.320
For me, it's very scary because if I see&nbsp;
someone in public with a gun, I will panic.

00:06:47.413 --> 00:06:49.531
That's going to send me into an anxiety attack.

00:06:49.531 --> 00:06:54.393
That constitutional carry law that&nbsp;
the state legislature passed in 2021&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.393 --> 00:06:57.131
had been rejected by Republican leaders.

00:06:57.131 --> 00:07:02.468
But as the Republican Party has gone further
and further to the right on issues

00:07:02.468 --> 00:07:07.729
you get a fringe of the&nbsp;
party that is much more vocal&nbsp;

00:07:07.729 --> 00:07:11.188
about all kinds of issues, including gun rights.

00:07:11.188 --> 00:07:14.881
In recent years, a better&nbsp;
organized gun control movement&nbsp;

00:07:14.881 --> 00:07:18.401
has seen more success with&nbsp;
tightening laws in some states.

00:07:18.401 --> 00:07:22.409
But the movement to expand&nbsp;
gun access isn’t stopping.

00:07:22.943 --> 00:07:27.583
In 2002 fewer than half of the 50&nbsp;
states had one party in control of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.583 --> 00:07:30.543
both the state legislature&nbsp;
and the governor’s office.

00:07:30.543 --> 00:07:33.348
Today, three quarters of the states do.

00:07:33.348 --> 00:07:37.785
That means, in the places where&nbsp;
Republicans or Democrats have full control

00:07:37.785 --> 00:07:41.654
they can push through new gun&nbsp;
laws with little chance of a veto.

00:07:41.654 --> 00:07:45.285
What happens and you see it&nbsp;
in state house to state house

00:07:45.285 --> 00:07:49.723
is one state passes a law that is very&nbsp;
successful for one side of the aisle.

00:07:49.723 --> 00:07:54.197
And then another state house&nbsp;
adopts a very, very similar law.

00:07:54.197 --> 00:07:57.477
Remember that constitutional carry law in Texas?

00:07:57.477 --> 00:08:01.326
Today, 24 states have similar&nbsp;
laws on the books for that, too.

00:08:01.326 --> 00:08:04.446
And more than 400 local governments across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.446 --> 00:08:09.246
20 states have adopted variations on&nbsp;
a “second amendment sanctuary law”

00:08:09.246 --> 00:08:12.769
meaning a city, town, or&nbsp;
county refuses to recognize&nbsp;

00:08:12.769 --> 00:08:14.529
any state or federal gun laws

00:08:14.529 --> 00:08:17.139
that they believe violate the Second Amendment.

00:08:17.139 --> 00:08:20.419
These things get replicated. They get&nbsp;
cloned, they go from state to state

00:08:20.419 --> 00:08:24.730
and they essentially make up this&nbsp;
patchwork of laws throughout the country.

00:08:26.415 --> 00:08:30.815
In June 2022, in the aftermath&nbsp;
of the Uvalde shooting in Texas

00:08:30.815 --> 00:08:34.895
President Biden signed the most&nbsp;
significant federal gun bill in 30 years.

00:08:36.485 --> 00:08:39.845
One thing it does is incentivize&nbsp;
states to pass red flag laws.

00:08:41.216 --> 00:08:42.976
But it can’t make them do it.

00:08:43.468 --> 00:08:46.925
That power still belongs to the states.

00:08:48.456 --> 00:08:51.042
I have survivor's guilt because I'm alive.

00:08:51.042 --> 00:08:56.082
And so I feel like I have to keep&nbsp;
speaking out. I have to do what I can.

00:08:56.082 --> 00:09:00.242
There's times when I just think I'm going to stop.

00:09:00.242 --> 00:09:03.768
I cannot do this for my own mental health.

00:09:03.768 --> 00:09:05.959
But we just keep, we keep going.

